Date:     Sat, 17 Apr 93 10:45:03 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Key Registration As Enslavement of the Press

I found Tom Morrow's review of the relevant clauses of the US Bill of Rights
helpful, but inexhaustive.

It would seem to me that the best ground for attacking mandatory crypto key
registration is freedom of the press.  I don't quite recall the precise
wording of this clause of Amendment I, but when it was written printing
presses were the forefront of information technology, much as computers are
now, and rapidly advancing.  Much of the economic growth in France in the
early 19th century was led by the printing industry, as the compuer industry
is leading US economic growth.

What would be the constitutional law status of a mandatory key registration
for any sort of hardcopy analogy to cryptography?

Basically, what this scheme would be saying is that the people won't be
free to express themselves in electronic publication without the intrusion
of the State.

The tough part about this is that cryptography is such an abstract issue.
I'm tring to think of analogies.  One possibility is that it's as if the
State required newspapers to register the combinations to any safe they kept
their records in with the State in the case that the State wanted to perform
a search.

Has the State ever tried to compel persons to disclose the combinations to
their safes?  What's the constitutional status of this?  I'd argue that
this would be self-incrimination, prohibited by the 5th amendment, giving
even more textual support to our position.  Has this been held up in court?

I'm going to have to see if I can call Mike Godwin of the EFF about this,
although he must be swamped.  He's their attorney, last I heard, and as I
recall, his e-mail address is something@eff.org - godwin@?  Anyone recall
exactly?  Is he on the cypherpunks list?

If it would be unconstitutional at present for safe combinations to be
registered with the State on a mandatory basis, then it would violate the
equal protection clause of the 14th amendment to single out cryptography.
That clause applies to all persons within US jurisdiction, not just citizens.

What a brainstorm!  What a nightmare!

