Date: Sat, 17 Apr 93 08:22:12 CDT
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: CRYPTO: The right to encrypt

Stanton McCandlish says:

>Thanks for that overview of the BoR. . . .
>One error I would like to point out:
>You say the 4th does not give the govt the right to conduct search & seizure,
>only that it specifies when they cannot.  This does not appear to be factual.
>Read again the line "But for [= "except in cases of"] probable cause."
>That very specifically enables the govt. to do s&s if they have "probable
>cause."

I do not agree.  A plain reading of the text of the 4th Amendment supports
my claim that the 4th does not explicitly give the feds power to conduct
searches and seizures.  Rather, it only specifies how they may *not*
conduct them.

Let's look at the text in full:  "The right of the people to be secure in
their persons, houses, papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches
and seizures, shall not be violated, and no warrants shall issue, but upon
probable cause, supported by oath or affirmation, and particularly
describing the place to be searched, and the persons or things to be
seized."

In case this isn't obvious, allow me to rephrase it:  "The right of the
people against unreasonsable searches and seizures shall not be violated,
and no warrant shall issue but upon probably cause."  Again, even more
simplified:  "The right shall not be violated, and no warrant shall issue
but upon probable cause."

How much clearer can I make it?  This obviously does not give the
government any new powers.  Rather, it only specifies a limitation on the
issuing of warrants. 

You apparently read this to give the government the power to conduct
reasonable searches and seizures with warrants issued upon probably cause. 
But that power can only be implied.  The 4th does not explicitly grant it. 

