Date: Sat, 17 Apr 93 08:30:13 CDT
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Key Registration As Enslavement of the Press

>It would seem to me that the best ground for attacking mandatory crypto key
>registration is freedom of the press.
>Tim Starr

While I welcome any useful means of defending our liberty to encrypt, I'm
less confident that the 1st Amendment's protection of the freedom of the
press will provide such a means.

First of all, the feds will argue that they are not trying to affect the
content of the press.  They are only trying to affect (assuming they ban
encryption) its mode of delivery.  But there are already many such
controls; newspapers cannot set up and use a private postal system, for
example.

Furthermore, I suspect that courts will find that the press does not have a
protectable interest in private encryption.  After all, it aims at
distributing information to the public, not hiding it in encryption.  

It's worth a try, of course, but I suspect that freedom of the press will
not prove as helpful as other provisions of the Bill of Rights in our
struggle to protect our rights to encryption technology.

